          %h2#Differentiation
            %strong Differentiation
          %p
            So far we have dealt with
            %em cohesive
            structures for which there is a notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %em smooth variation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            along a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %em differentiation
            is to measure the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/difference"} difference
            between the position of two points on a cohesive trajectory in space as the difference between their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            coordinates “tends to 0” without actually becoming 0. One also says that differentiation is forming “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
            differences” of a cohesive process – and we will make precise here what this means.
          %p There are two stages to the theory of differentiation:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                We may think of differentiation as just a means to analyze more in detail the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive structure
                already given, without adding new
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+object"} line object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                in a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%E2%88%9E-topos"} cohesive ∞-topos
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ω
                        %mi cl
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbf{d} \colon \mathbb{R} \to \Omega^1_{cl}(-,\mathbb{R})
              %p
                from the line to the cohesive moduli space of closed
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
                %em differential
                of the curve, its rate of infinitesimal change at that point.
              %p
                Below in
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#DifferentiationOfSmoothFunctionsAndDifferentialForms"} Differentiation of smooth functions and differetial forms
                we discuss this construction in the standard model of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth cohesion
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}
              %p
                Further below in
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#CohesiveDifferentiation"} Maurer-Cartan forms – Cohesive differentiation
                we show how
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}
                comes out from just the abstract axioms of cohesionn.
            %li
              %p
                We may think of differentiation as reflecting a refinement of smooth cohesion such that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimal
                cohesive trajectories actually exist. Here, on top of having a
                %em measure
                for how a cohesive trajectory changes infinitesimally at a given point, it makes sense to ask concretely if two points on a trajectory are infinitesimally close to each other. In this approach the very notion of cohesion is refined to include
                %em explicitly
                a way to speak not just about a “cohesive blob of points”, but to decide whether it is in fact just an “infinitesimal cohesive blob of points” – an
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} Differential geometry
                with such an explicit notion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+space"} infinitesimals
                is known as
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
                here allow one to
                %em synthesize
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimally+thickened+point"} infinitesimally thickened point
                and not just to reason about it
                %em as if
                it existed.
              %p
                In such a synthetic differential context then the differential
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}
                from above not just exists as a whole, but we can “take it apart and re-synthesize it” by realizing its value at each point literally as the ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/difference"} difference
                between two
                %em infinitesimally close
                points. Similarly, various other fundamental constructions in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundles"} tangent bundles
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
                have a usefully transparent axiomatic characterization in the presence of synthetic infinitesimals. (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sophus+Lie"}> Sophus Lie
                , one of the founding fathers of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
                %a{:href => "synthetic+differential+geometry#SophusLieQuote"} famously said
                that he indeed found his theorems using such synthetic reasoning intuitively, and just did not publish them this way due to a lack of formalization of this language – at his time. ) This we discuss in the Mod Layer in
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#DGeometry"} D-geometry
                below.
          %p
            In the
            %a{:href => "#DifferentiationLayerSem"} Differentiation semantic layer
            below we formalize differentiation, and these two aspects of it, by adding to the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            that of an
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+cohesion"} infinitesimal cohesive neighbourhood
          %p
            Recalling that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+topos"} cohesive topos
            is an abstract
            = succeed "," do
              %em cohesive blob
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
